Summary: d3dx9: put at least one character on each line in DrawText, so a word break can't loop
Category: graphics
Kind: fix
Origin: wine-mac-builds
  Note: dlls/d3dx9_36/font.c, word_break()
Upstream: no
Why: With DT_WORDBREAK, ID3DXFont::DrawText breaks each line in
 word_break(). When not even the line's first character fits the width,
 it used no characters at all, so read_line() started the next line at the
 same place, and DrawText never returned with DT_CALCRECT or DT_NOCLIP.
 Now at least one character (a surrogate pair counts as one) goes on the
 line, as in user32's DrawText. Wine 11 loads its builtin d3dx9_30 where
 10.0 loaded the copy a game ships (upstream dropped --prefer-native from
 the d3dx9 DLLs), so games that worked on 10.0 reach this code.
Programs: Nancy Drew: The White Wolf of Icicle Creek: loading a save at the
 peg-board puzzle hangs (Game.exe at 100% CPU) on Wine 11, where the game
 lays out the word "is" of a subtitle in a rectangle 3 pixels wide
Uncertain: what native d3dx9 returns for a rectangle narrower than one
 character: the game's own d3dx9_30.dll doesn't hang there, and user32's
 DrawText puts one character on each line, but Windows wasn't tested. A
 conformance test in dlls/d3dx9_36/tests would need that checked first.
Status: 11.22: 2026-11-27: tests/regress/d3dx9-wordbreak-narrow fails on
 macfixes 11.22-build0 and passes in a quick build with this patch; White
 Wolf's peg-board save loads there 1 of 1 (build0 hung 2 of 2). Not in a
 full build; Windows not tested.
 11.21: 2026-11-27: applies unchanged; the test fails on 11.21-build0 and
 passes in a quick build. Not in a full build.
 10.0: 2026-11-27: applies unchanged; the test fails on 10.0-build0 and
 passes in a quick build. 10.0 loads a game's own d3dx9 (--prefer-native).
 Not in a full build.

diff --git a/dlls/d3dx9_36/font.c b/dlls/d3dx9_36/font.c
--- a/dlls/d3dx9_36/font.c
+++ b/dlls/d3dx9_36/font.c
@@ -538,8 +538,13 @@
     if (!sla[i].fSoftBreak)
         i = chars_fit;
 
+    /* Put at least one character on the line, even if it doesn't fit, or a
+     * character wider than the line is never passed. */
+    if (!i && !sla[0].fWhiteSpace)
+        i = (*str_len > 1 && IS_HIGH_SURROGATE(str[0]) && IS_LOW_SURROGATE(str[1])) ? 2 : 1;
+
     *chars_used = i;
-    if (sla[i].fWhiteSpace)
+    if (i < *str_len && sla[i].fWhiteSpace)
         ++(*chars_used);
 
     /* Remove extra spaces */
